00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:16.020
Today I am going to show you the cleaning
and repair of two specimens. First of all,

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:20.980
we have this frog. Look how cute this frog
is. It was on display in the Mammal Hall here

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:25.360
in the museum, in a place where it was a bit
hidden. The fun about this was that the visitors,

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:29.700
as they were walking, could say "What is that
doing there that is not a mammal?" So, well,

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:34.570
when it was on display, this wooden base that
it has was covered in these little red pebbles,

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:40.870
right? So now the whole base is all with this
kinda reddish pink powder. So, the idea is

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:54.460
that we are going to clean this frog a little,
so that it is in better condition to be able

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:57.809
to study it or perhaps bring it back on display.
What we are doing, then, in the beginning,

00:00:57.809 --> 00:01:05.230
is simply taking out all these pebbles, which
we don't want. But some of them are in places

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:12.560
a bit complicated, so we are moving them a
little here, a little there ... There it is,

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:18.300
and it is getting better and better, the poor
frog, without all these pebbles that are in

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:26.970
the way and don't let us see what's going
on with the bones, right? Because the idea

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:35.640
of having this animal is
to be able to see its bones. So, we continue

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:41.770
to remove the pebbles a little, and after
removing the pebbles what we are going to

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:49.730
do, a little with this brush that I have in
my hand, we are going to remove this dust

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:56.900
that we do not like at all. So, there we are
removing it. And another technique that we

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:03.610
sometimes use to clean is the same sponges
that I already showed you in the video where

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:08.810
I was cleaning the horse, so make sure you
watch that video because it's great how all

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:15.250
the dust that a horse skull had, comes off,
in the same series of videos; don't miss

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:22.530
it. And there, more or less, you can see how
it turns out. Of course I am not going to

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:29.620
finish it here because there is a lot of work
to do, but it is a first approximation of

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:35.080
how this poor frog will look like, who had
all this red dust and was a little dirty and

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:43.960
was not happy at all, or cheerful at all.
So, another thing that I'm going to show you

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:49.910
today, besides cleaning this frog, is another
specimen that was also in the Mammal Hall,

00:02:49.910 --> 00:03:02.900
which is a little deer. I have it next to
me and now I'm going to show it to you, when

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:07.480
I finish getting a little more of this dust
out. There it is ... so, this is a first approximation,

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:11.260
obviously it will not be like that, it will
be much cleaner when I finish working with

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:17.690
the frog, but, as a first step, the idea is
to remove all this that was next to the frog,

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:21.050
which we don't want. So, at least now the
bones are not at risk, and we can continue

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:23.960
cleaning this specimen, calmly.
Let's now talk about this little deer, which,

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:35.020
as I told you, was also on display in the
Mammal Hall. We are going to leave the frog

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:44.650
on one side... and what happened to it during
the time it was on display is that one of

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:54.190
its fingers came off. And here you can see
that I collected that finger. There is the

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:58.820
little finger I collected. So, the idea, what
are we going to do today. First, we are

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:03.400
going to take out a little bit of these pebbles
that are here because they are in the way.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:16.139
See how the leg moves? It is loose. We are
going to put a little protection here. And

00:04:16.139 --> 00:04:20.370
we are going to use an adhesive that we refer
to as "archival", a conservation adhesive,

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:24.010
and what we are going to do is use it, slowly,
with this little finger. Here you can see

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:35.980
it. We are going to wet the brush and put
the adhesive on the finger. And now, slowly,

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:44.820
very carefully, we are going to stick this
finger back. There you have it, how the deer

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:53.660
looks with its little finger stuck back on.
Well, the idea was to show you a bit of the

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:59.690
conservation work that we do here at the museum,
with a little cleaning and with a little repair

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:04.600
of some specimens. As I told you, we also
have a preventive conservation video, don't

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:10.730
miss it. And in addition, we have another
video about cleaning and repairing feathers

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:12.680
from taxidermy animals. Thank you very much!

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:13.680
 

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:19.680
